Object.assign(ARTICLES, {"fmw-goto-chair-pogo-0720": {"p": "fmw", "kicker": "FMW · Summer tour, Night 2 · Konan, July 20", "title": "Goto's Chair, Pogo's Head, Onita's Win", "dek": "Tarzan Goto's chair hits his own partner, Onita steals the main event, and the Sheik turns on his nephew with fire.", "by": "By the magazine staff", "date": "1991-07-20", "factTitle": "The Card", "facts": [["Main", "Onita & Verichev d. Goto & Pogo"], ["Match of the night", "Big Titan d. Sambo Asako"], ["Crowd", "2,654"]], "body": [["h", "Goto's Chair, Pogo's Head, Onita's Win"], ["p", "Tarzan Goto came back to FMW on Saturday night after a day in Sapporo with New Japan, and by the end of the night he might not have a place to come back to. Goto and Mr. Pogo met Atsushi Onita and Grigory Verichev in the main event, and for seventeen minutes Pogo's side had it their way. Then Goto picked up a steel chair and swung it at Onita. Onita ducked. The chair caught Pogo square on the head, and while Goto stood there staring at what he had done, Onita rolled him up for three at 17:53."], ["p", "Goto lay on the mat after the three count with Pogo down beside him. Nobody from Pogo's side came to help either of them up. On Friday, Pogo told the crowd that Goto chose New Japan. On Saturday, Goto's chair chose for him."], ["h", "Titan Chokeslams Asako"], ["p", "Big Titan and Sambo Asako tore the building apart for over fifteen minutes in the best match on the card. No countouts, no disqualifications, and the two of them used every inch of the gym. Asako kept coming back, but at 15:38 Titan caught him by the throat, lifted him up and drove him down with the chokeslam for the pin."], ["h", "The Sheik Turns on His Nephew"], ["p", "Nobody in Konan expected to see The Sheik and Sabu on opposite sides of the ring. Sabu stood with Ricky Fuji. The Sheik walked out with Horace Boulder, one of Pogo's men. It ended at 11:58 when the Sheik locked his own nephew in the camel clutch and Sabu had no choice but to give up. The Sheik wasn't finished. He reached into his trunks and threw fire at Sabu as he lay on the mat, and Fuji had to drag him out of the ring. Whatever happened between the two of them after Sagamihara, it is now personal."], ["h", "Tsuchiya and Nakamura Win the Women's Tag"], ["p", "Eriko Tsuchiya and Rie Nakamura beat Keiko Iwami and Yukie Nabeno when Tsuchiya caught Nabeno with the asai leg lariat at 11:03. Nakamura is now 2-0 on the tour, with both wins over Nabeno."], ["h", "Honda Opens with a Win"], ["p", "Masashi Honda pinned Yukihide Ueno with a bodytrap cradle at 7:44 to open the show."], ["p", "NEXT: Kochi, Monday, July 22. A No-DQ Bunkhouse Brawl: Mr. Pogo, Big Titan and Horace Boulder against Atsushi Onita, Sambo Asako and Ricky Fuji."]]}, "fmw-observer-konan-0720": {"p": "fmw", "series": "observer", "news": 1, "by": "Wrestling Observer Newsletter", "date": "1991-07-20", "region": "Japan Report", "kicker": "FMW · Konan · 7/20", "title": "Five Matches and a Business", "dek": "Konan draws 1,100 more than Sagamihara on half the payroll, Goto's chair splits the heel side, and the Sheik turns on Sabu.", "factTitle": "Match observations", "facts": [["Venue", "Aichi Konan Citizen Gym"], ["City", "Konan, Aichi"], ["Date", "July 20, 1991"], ["Attendance", "2,654"], ["Average ticket", "$10.00"], ["Gate", "$26,540"], ["Merchandise", "$1,327"], ["Card", "*"], ["Match of the night", "Titan vs. Asako, ***1/4"]], "body": [["p", "FMW drew 2,654 in Konan on Saturday at a $10 average for $26,540, with $1,327 in merchandise at 50 cents a head. That's 1,100 more people and $11,000 more at the gate than the opener in Sagamihara the night before, and it's the number that matters most for this company right now. The other number that matters went the right way too. Sagamihara used 28 wrestlers and the payroll ate almost two-thirds of the gate. Konan used 16 on a five-match card. The talent bill should come in well under half of the house, which is the first FMW show you could call a business. The card rated *, and nobody was hurt."], ["p", "The card was cut to five matches, and the names who weren't on it tell the story. No Leon Spinks, no Kevin Sullivan, no Shooter, no Soviets outside Verichev, and none of the foreign women. Two of Sagamihara's four worst matches were the different-style fights, and after one night the office has already taken them off the card. That's the right call. The question is what Spinks and Sullivan are for if they're not booked on a Saturday."], ["p", "The main event went 17:53 and rated *. Atsushi Onita and Grigory Verichev beat Tarzan Goto and Mr. Pogo when Goto swung a chair at Onita, hit Pogo instead, and Onita rolled Goto up. The match was mostly brawling on the floor and never caught fire, but the finish is the most important angle FMW has. Goto works New Japan on the nights Pogo carries the heel side, and he's back at New Japan in Sendai on Monday. Pogo told the crowd in Sagamihara that Goto chose New Japan. Goto came to Konan and cost Pogo the match. The heel side of this company now has two leaders, and they just hurt each other. Verichev needed a win after The Shooter tapped him on Friday, and he got to stand on the winning side of the main event."], ["p", "The best match on the card was Big Titan over Sambo Asako with the chokeslam at 15:38, ***1/4, in a no countout, no DQ match. That's the only FMW match on either night to crack ***, and it went fifteen minutes on a card where nothing else got above one star. Titan is big, moves well and works the crowd, and Asako takes a beating as well as anyone on the roster. Titan should be getting more time, not less."], ["p", "The Sheik and Horace Boulder beat Sabu and Ricky Fuji at 11:58 when the Sheik made Sabu submit to the camel clutch, and then threw fire at him. 3/4*. One night after they teamed together in Sagamihara, the Sheik turned on his own nephew and walked out with one of Pogo's men. Nobody is saying why, and Sabu isn't talking. As a match it was a mess, but the angle is strong. The Sheik is 65 and can't carry a match, so Sabu gets an uncle to chase and a reason to go after Pogo's side, and the Sheik gets to do the only thing he can still do, which is throw the fire."], ["p", "The two undercard matches were among the worst on the tour so far. Eriko Tsuchiya and Rie Nakamura beat Keiko Iwami and Yukie Nabeno at -***1/4 when Tsuchiya pinned Nabeno with the asai leg lariat. Nakamura is 2-0, both over Nabeno. Masashi Honda pinned Yukihide Ueno with a bodytrap cradle in 7:44 in the opener, -***. These are rookies wrestling rookies, and it shows."], ["p", "1. Masashi Honda pinned Yukihide Ueno with a bodytrap cradle in 7:44. -***"], ["p", "2. Eriko Tsuchiya & Rie Nakamura beat Keiko Iwami & Yukie Nabeno in 11:03 when Tsuchiya pinned Nabeno with the asai leg lariat. -***1/4"], ["p", "3. The Sheik & Horace Boulder beat Sabu & Ricky Fuji in 11:58 when the Sheik made Sabu submit to the camel clutch. Fire at Sabu after. 3/4*"], ["p", "4. No countout, no DQ: Big Titan pinned Sambo Asako with the chokeslam in 15:38. Match of the night. ***1/4"], ["p", "5. Atsushi Onita & Grigory Verichev beat Tarzan Goto & Mr. Pogo in 17:53 when Onita rolled up Goto, after Goto's chair hit Pogo. *"], ["p", "Overall card: *"], ["p", "Next is Kochi on Monday, a no-DQ bunkhouse brawl: Pogo, Titan and Boulder against Onita, Asako and Fuji. Goto is in Sendai with New Japan."]]}, "fmw-diary-sabu-0720": {"p": "fmw", "series": "diary", "by": "Sabu", "date": "1991-07-20", "place": "The tour bus, Konan", "kicker": "From the Road · Konan, 7/20", "title": "He Put It on Me", "dek": "Sabu on finding his uncle on the other side, the camel clutch, and the fire.", "factTitle": "The Night", "facts": [["Show", "Summer tour, Night 2"], ["Match", "Sabu & Fuji vs. Sheik & Boulder"], ["Finish", "Camel clutch, 11:58"], ["Rating", "3/4*"], ["Next", "Kochi, 7/22"]], "body": [["p", "Last night in Sagamihara I stood on the apron next to my uncle and watched him put Ueda to sleep with the camel clutch. Tonight he put it on me."], ["p", "I found out we were on opposite sides when I got to the building. Fuji was in the dressing room taping his back from the table, and he looked at the sheet on the wall and then at me and said, \"You're with me tonight.\" I asked where my uncle was. Fuji pointed down the hall. My uncle was sitting with Boulder and the rest of Pogo's people, with his bag at his feet. He didn't look up when I walked past."], ["p", "I've known that man my whole life. He taught me to fall. He taught me that the people in the cheap seats came to see something they'll never see anywhere else. I don't know what changed between last night and this afternoon, and he didn't tell me."], ["p", "In the ring he didn't say a word either. He just worked. Boulder did most of the damage, and I did what I do, off the top, off the chairs, over the guardrail. Fuji fought hurt the whole way. At the end my uncle got me down in the middle of the ring, sat on my back and pulled. I've seen him do it to a hundred men. I didn't think he'd do it to me. I held on as long as I could and then I couldn't."], ["p", "I was still on the mat when I felt the heat. Fire. I've been around it my whole life and it never gets less scary. Fuji grabbed me under the arms and dragged me out before the second one came. My eyebrows are mostly still there."], ["p", "Onita came and found me in the back. He didn't say anything about my uncle. He just told me I'm on his side now, and that in this company you pick a side and you live with it. I think I picked one tonight. I don't think I had much choice."], ["p", "Kochi on Monday. My uncle won't be on the bus with me anymore."]]}});

